.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt APREFD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aprefd
.Nd collect recorded file reads and update the prefetch profiles
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dMn
.Op Fl k Ar keep
.Op Fl m Ar min
.Nm
.Fl s
.Op Fl dMn
.Op Fl i Ar idle
.Op Fl k Ar keep
.Op Fl m Ar min
.Op Fl t Ar limit
.Ar uid session
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the access entries that
.Xr aprefetch 4
records during the boot, until the boot recording window closes at the
first login or on timeout.
It then saves them as a trace,
.Pa /var/db/aprefetch/boot.trace ,
renaming the older traces to
.Pa boot.trace.1 ,
.Pa boot.trace.2
and so on, and rebuilds the boot profile
.Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof
from the newest traces, and exits.
.Pp
With
.Fl s ,
.Nm
records a login session instead: the session named
.Ar session ,
such as a session of
.Xr akture 8 ,
of the user whose user ID is
.Ar uid .
It detaches at once, opens a recording window, and closes it once no read
has missed the cache for
.Ar idle
seconds, which is when the session has read what it needs to start, or
after
.Ar limit
seconds.
The trace is saved as
.Pa /var/db/aprefetch/session/ Ns Ar uid Ns - Ns Ar session Ns Pa .trace ,
the older ones renamed as for the boot, and the profile is rebuilt in the
same way, as
.Ar uid Ns - Ns Ar session Ns Pa .prof
in the same directory, for
.Ic aprefctl replay
to replay at the next login.
A session name is made of letters, digits,
.Ql + ,
.Ql - ,
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql _ ,
and does not start with a dot.
.Pp
Each recording window starts out with the entries left from the last one
discarded, so only one
.Nm
reads the device at a time: a session waits up to ten seconds for the
.Nm
that reads the boot window to finish, and is not recorded if it does
not.
A window records the reads of every process, not only those of the
session.
.Pp
The profile keeps the pages, and the metadata blocks, that were read in at
least
.Ar min
of the newest
.Ar keep
traces, or in all of them while there are fewer, so that work done in only
one boot or session does not make the profile grow.
The pages are grouped into ranges of a file, and the ranges and the blocks
are ordered by the time at which the newest trace that has them read them
first, so that when the budget of the replay runs out, what was read early
is kept.
The profile is limited to 16 MB and to the eight file systems with the most
pages.
.Pp
Both files are written to a temporary name and renamed into place, with
mode 0600; the directory of the sessions is made with mode 0700.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground and report to the standard error instead of
.Xr syslog 3 .
.It Fl i Ar idle
With
.Fl s ,
close the window once no read has missed the cache for this many
seconds; 3 by default.
.It Fl k Ar keep
Keep and use this many traces, from 1 to 9; 3 by default.
.It Fl m Ar min
A page must be in this many traces; 2 by default.
.It Fl M
Leave the metadata blocks out of the profile, so that the replay reads
file data only.
This is to measure what prefetching metadata gains.
.It Fl n
Save the trace but leave the profile alone.
With no profile, this records without prefetching.
.It Fl s
Record a login session, as described above.
.It Fl t Ar limit
With
.Fl s ,
close the window after this many seconds at most; 30 by default.
The window also closes after
.Va kern.aprefetch.record_timeout
seconds.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
is started at boot by
.Pa /etc/rc.d/aprefd
when
.Va aprefd
is set in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
which is the default.
.Pp
At a login on the console,
.Xr pam_exec 8
runs
.Pa /usr/libexec/aprefetch-login
from
.Pa /etc/pam.d/login
once the user has been let in.
It closes the boot window, starts
.Nm Fl s
with the session name
.Dq login ,
and replays the profile of the last console session of the user.
For a graphical login, the aprefetch hook of
.Xr akture 8
does the same, and replays the profile of the last session of the last
user while the password is typed.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/db/aprefetch/session -compact
.It Pa /dev/aprefetch
.It Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof
.It Pa /usr/libexec/aprefetch-login
.It Pa /var/db/aprefetch/boot.trace
.It Pa /var/db/aprefetch/session
the traces and profiles of the sessions
.It Pa /var/run/aprefd.pid
the process ID of
.Nm
reading the boot window
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
To measure a boot that records but does not prefetch, remove the profile
and keep
.Nm
from making a new one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# aprefctl delete boot
# echo 'aprefd_flags="-n"' >> /etc/rc.conf
.Ed
.Pp
To see how the window of a session would close, record one in the
foreground, here the session
.Dq test
of user ID 1000, without a profile:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# aprefd -d -n -s 1000 test
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aprefetch 4 ,
.Xr akture 8 ,
.Xr aprefctl 8 ,
.Xr pam_exec 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written for AutumnBSD.
